     "page": 1,
     "text": "CLINICAL SUMMARY - Jones Gonzalez and Williams Medical Group\nPatient: Thomas Edwards DOB: 07/26/1977 Sex: M MRN: 135983\n\nEncounters:\n 2025-02-07 lab review BP 108/66 Wt 144\n 2023-06-29 flu shot BP 105/76 Wt 148\n 2021-11-16 Multiple sclerosis BP 119/71 Wt 147\n\nAssessment/Plan:\n• Multiple sclerosis (EDSS 2) - controlled; meds: natalizumab 300 mg IV, fingolimod 0.5 mg\n\nOn February 7, 2025, Mr. Thomas Edwards, a 47-year-old male with a history of multiple sclerosis (MS), presented for a routine lab review. His blood pressure at the time of this visit was 108/66 mmHg and his weight was 144 pounds. Mr. Edwards is currently with intravenous natalizumab 300 mg and fingolimod 0.5 mg. His MS is well-controlled with an Expanded Disability Status Scale (EDSS) score of 2, indicating no walking impairment and full functional capacity in all systems.\n\nMr. Edwards continues to engage in regular physical activity, running three to four times a week. His lifestyle habits have notably improved as he has quit smoking, which he used to do at a rate of 10 cigarettes per day starting from June 1994, and stopped smoking on December 20, 2022. His smoking cessation was accompanied by a comprehensive discussion about the benefits of quitting and strategies for maintaining this healthy change. Regarding alcohol consumption, Mr. Edwards reports having approximately six drinks per week, which is considered within moderate limits.\n\nDuring the June 29, 2023, visit, Mr. Edwards received a flu shot. His blood pressure that time was 105/76 mmHg and his weight was 148 pounds. His neurological exam showed no new deficits and he remains stable on his current medication regimen.\n\nIn November 2021, Mr. Edwards was seen for a follow-up regarding his multiple sclerosis. His blood pressure was recorded at 119/71 mmHg and his weight at 147 pounds. He reported no new symptoms, and his neurological examination was consistent with his baseline status. The patient continues to manage his condition well with his current therapeutic regimen of natalizumab and fingolimod.\n\nAdditional routine findings from recent exams include normal vital signs, a well-healed surgical scar from a previous minor procedure, and no significant abnormalities noted in the cardiovascular, respiratory, or gastrointestinal There have been no new complaints of headaches, dizziness, or visual disturbances, which are common symptoms associated with MS. \n\nMr. Edwards' last MRI and routine blood work, conducted as part of ongoing MS management, have shown no new lesions or significant abnorma"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "lities. He has remained compliant with his treatment plan and has not reported any side effects from his medications. His lifestyle modifications, including regular exercise and a moderate alcohol intake, contribute to his overall well-being management of chronic conditions.\n\nOverall, Mr. Edwards is stable and compliant with his treatment regimen. His multiple sclerosis is well-controlled with an EDSS score of Z, and he continues to lead an active lifestyle with regular physical activity. His cardiovascular risk factors are managed, and his social history reflects a commitment to healthier habits.\n"
    }
